// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Environmental science studies how living things and their environment interact, and how humans affect both.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An ecosystem includes living (biotic) and nonliving (abiotic) parts. Energy enters most ecosystems as sunlight, captured by producers through photosynthesis."},{"t":"key","text":"Energy flows one way through food chains and webs: producers → primary consumers → secondary consumers → tertiary consumers. About 10% of energy passes to each next level; the rest is lost as heat."},{"t":"p","text":"Decomposers break down dead material, returning nutrients to the soil. Because so much energy is lost, there are far fewer top predators than producers, which is shown by energy pyramids."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Energy pyramid","aim":"Follow 10,000 kJ of plant energy up a food chain. How much reaches each trophic level?","sets":[{"name":"n","start":2,"label":"Trophic level","min":1,"max":5,"step":1},{"name":"eff","start":10,"label":"Transfer efficiency (%)","min":5,"max":20,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"E","expr":"10000*(eff/100)^(n - 1)","label":"Energy available (kJ)"}],"steps":["Record the energy at all 5 trophic levels.","Try a higher efficiency. How many levels can the chain support?"],"noise":0,"result":"Only about 10% passes to each level; the rest is lost as heat from respiration. That's why food chains rarely have more than 4–5 levels and top predators are rare.","plot":{"x":"n","y":"E"}}]},{"say":"Matter cycles through ecosystems over and over. And Earth's climate creates distinct biomes.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The carbon, nitrogen, water and phosphorus cycles move matter between living things and the environment. Humans alter them, for example by burning fossil fuels (carbon) and using fertilizer (nitrogen and phosphorus)."},{"t":"p","text":"Biomes are large regions with similar climates and communities:"},{"t":"list","items":["Tropical rainforest: hot, wet, highest biodiversity.","Desert: very dry.","Grassland, temperate deciduous forest, taiga (boreal forest), and tundra (cold, frozen permafrost).","Aquatic biomes: freshwater, estuaries, coral reefs and the open ocean."]},{"t":"p","text":"Climate (temperature and precipitation) is the main factor that determines a biome."}]}],[{"say":"Populations grow and shrink based on resources. Human population growth is one of the biggest environmental factors.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Exponential growth (J-curve) happens with unlimited resources. Logistic growth (S-curve) levels off at the carrying capacity, the largest population an environment can support."},{"t":"p","text":"Limiting factors like food, water, space, predators and disease keep populations in check."},{"t":"p","text":"The human population has grown to over 8 billion. Demographic transition: as countries develop, death rates fall first, then birth rates fall, slowing growth."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A deer population grows quickly, then levels off at 500. What does 500 represent?","steps":["Growth stops when resources limit the population"],"answer":"The carrying capacity"},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Population projection","aim":"Project a population of 8 billion forward at different growth rates.","sets":[{"name":"r","start":1,"label":"Growth rate (% per year)","min":0.1,"max":4,"step":0.1},{"name":"t","start":50,"label":"Years","min":0,"max":100,"step":5}],"outs":[{"name":"P","expr":"8*exp(r/100*t)","label":"Population (billions)"},{"name":"Td","expr":"70/r","label":"Doubling time (years)"}],"steps":["Record the population after 0, 25, 50, 75 and 100 years at 1% growth.","Compare the doubling time at 2% and 0.5%."],"noise":1,"result":"Exponential growth adds more people each year even at a constant rate. The rule of 70 gives the doubling time: 70 years at 1%, but only 35 years at 2%.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"P"}}]},{"say":"Biodiversity, the variety of life, makes ecosystems strong. Today it faces serious threats.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Biodiversity includes genetic diversity, species diversity and ecosystem diversity. Diverse ecosystems are more stable and provide services like clean water, pollination and medicine."},{"t":"key","text":"Major threats to biodiversity (HIPPCO): Habitat loss, Invasive species, Pollution, Population growth, Climate change and Overharvesting. Habitat loss is the leading cause of extinction."},{"t":"p","text":"Conservation strategies include protected areas, wildlife corridors connecting habitats, laws like the Endangered Species Act, and restoring damaged ecosystems."},{"t":"p","text":"A keystone species has an outsized role; removing it can collapse an ecosystem (like sea otters in kelp forests)."}]}],[{"say":"Fresh water, healthy soil and land are limited resources that people depend on every day.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Agriculture uses about 70% of fresh water worldwide. Groundwater in aquifers can be depleted when pumped faster than it recharges."},{"t":"key","text":"Sustainable agriculture protects soil and water: crop rotation, cover crops, no-till farming, drip irrigation and integrated pest management."},{"t":"p","text":"Deforestation clears forests for farms and cities, causing erosion, habitat loss and more CO₂ in the air. Urban sprawl spreads cities outward, increasing car use and paving over habitats."},{"t":"ex","problem":"An aquifer refills at 2 billion gallons a year, but farms pump out 5 billion. What happens?","steps":["Water is removed faster than it's replaced"],"answer":"The water table drops, wells may go dry, and the land can sink"}]},{"say":"How we make energy affects air quality, climate and our future. Let's compare the options.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Nonrenewable sources: coal, oil, natural gas (fossil fuels) and nuclear power. Renewable sources: solar, wind, hydroelectric, geothermal and biomass."},{"t":"list","items":["Fossil fuels are reliable and energy-dense but release CO₂ and air pollutants.","Nuclear power produces no CO₂ while operating but creates radioactive waste.","Solar and wind produce no emissions while running but depend on weather, so they need storage like batteries.","Hydroelectric dams provide steady power but disrupt rivers and fish migration."]},{"t":"p","text":"Energy conservation and efficiency (LED bulbs, insulation, efficient cars) are often the cheapest ways to reduce environmental impact."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Solar panel test","aim":"Measure how much energy a rooftop solar array produces each day.","sets":[{"name":"A","start":20,"label":"Panel area (m²)","min":1,"max":40,"step":1},{"name":"eff","start":20,"label":"Panel efficiency (%)","min":10,"max":25,"step":1},{"name":"sun","start":5,"label":"Peak sun hours per day","min":2,"max":8,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"E","expr":"A*1*eff/100*sun","label":"Energy per day (kWh)"}],"steps":["Record daily energy for at least 4 array sizes.","Compare a sunny climate (7 hours) with a cloudy one (3 hours)."],"noise":5,"result":"Output scales with area, efficiency and sunlight. A typical home uses about 30 kWh a day, so roughly 30 m² of panels can cover it in a sunny place.","plot":{"x":"A","y":"E"}}]}],[{"say":"Pollution harms people and ecosystems. Understanding its sources is the first step to solving it.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Air pollution: burning fossil fuels releases particulates, sulfur and nitrogen oxides (causing acid rain) and forms smog. Indoor air pollution (like radon) also matters.","Water pollution: point sources come from one place (a factory pipe); nonpoint sources come from many places (runoff from farms and streets).","Eutrophication: fertilizer runoff causes algae blooms that use up oxygen, creating dead zones.","Land pollution: solid waste in landfills, plastic and hazardous waste."]},{"t":"key","text":"Biomagnification: toxins like mercury and DDT become more concentrated at higher levels of the food chain, harming top predators."},{"t":"p","text":"The Clean Air Act and Clean Water Act regulate pollution in the US. Reduce, reuse and recycle cut waste."}]},{"say":"Climate change is one of the biggest challenges of our time, but there are many ways to address it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Greenhouse gases (CO₂, methane, nitrous oxide) trap heat. Since the Industrial Revolution, burning fossil fuels and deforestation have raised CO₂ levels and global temperatures."},{"t":"p","text":"Effects: rising sea levels, melting ice, ocean acidification (oceans absorbing CO₂), more intense heat waves and storms, and shifting habitats."},{"t":"p","text":"Mitigation reduces emissions: renewable energy, efficiency, electric vehicles and protecting forests. Adaptation prepares for changes: sea walls, drought-resistant crops and better planning."},{"t":"p","text":"International agreements like the Paris Agreement set goals to limit warming. Individuals can help by conserving energy and making sustainable choices."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"CO₂ and global temperature","aim":"Use a climate model to estimate warming for different atmospheric CO₂ levels.","sets":[{"name":"C","start":420,"label":"CO₂ (ppm)","min":280,"max":1000,"step":10},{"name":"S","start":3,"label":"Climate sensitivity (°C per doubling)","min":2,"max":4.5,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"dT","expr":"S*log2(C/280)","label":"Warming since 1850 (°C)"}],"steps":["Record the warming at 280 (pre-industrial), 420 (today), 560 and 840 ppm.","Change the sensitivity to see the range scientists consider likely."],"noise":0,"result":"Each doubling of CO₂ adds about the same warming (the effect is logarithmic). Today's 420 ppm already commits us to about 1.7 °C eventually; 560 ppm would bring about 3 °C.","plot":{"x":"C","y":"dT"}}]}]];
export default text;
